# Manager UI

One user: a campaign manager, mid-race, under time pressure, often on a phone, frequently not a data person. She opens Wings with a question and wants it answered in under ten seconds.

## Six screens

Campaign Setup, Path to Victory, Program & Budget, Adopt Plan, Command Center, Reforecast. Imports, turnout detail, settings, evidence, and audit support these but never become competing primary destinations.

## Progressive disclosure

Three levels. Putting Level 3 content on Level 1 is the most common failure here.

- **Level 1 — the answer.** Status, adopted goal, actual through date, and projected gap when a gap exists. Nothing a manager would not act on today.
- **Level 2 — cause and response.** Percent complete, required pace, recent pace, projected final, the triggering alert in plain English, available adjustments.
- **Level 3 — the audit.** Formulas, metric keys, dependencies, assumption keys and evidence classes, modeled vs adopted values, override history, engine and calibration versions.

Level 2 explains in sentences, not formulas. "25,694 people are expected to vote. You need half of them, plus one." Metric keys, formula strings, and version numbers appear at Level 3 and nowhere above it.

For a capacity gap, Level 1 leads with additional workers and incremental cost. Attempts, completed shifts, scheduled shifts, and daily pace go to Level 2.

## Status

Four states. `UNAVAILABLE` from the engine displays as NO DATA with a hollow gray ring — never a filled dot, never green, never a performance state.

- ON TRACK — green dot
- WATCH — amber dot
- AT RISK — red dot
- NO DATA — hollow gray ring

Every status is tappable and names its triggering rule. No health score, no gauge, no percentage, no composite.

## Plan construction

Screens edit parts of a draft. They NEVER build a `PlanVersionRecord` themselves — that is `builder.ts` in `@wings/plan-domain`. A screen that serializes its own plan will silently overwrite inputs owned by other screens.

Route adoption errors using the structured context on `PlanAdoptionError`. Do not parse error strings and do not maintain a gap-to-screen map in the UI.

## Visual system

Five decisions, no exceptions. Consistency is what makes conventional software look considered.

- One typeface, weights 400 and 500 only. Never 600 or 700.
- All figures tabular (`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`). Every column of numbers must align.
- Spacing scale: 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48. Every gap is one of these.
- Achromatic surfaces and text. The four status colors are the only saturated elements on any screen.
- Motion under 200ms, causality only. Numbers NEVER animate on load or count up.

Layout follows conventional dashboard patterns deliberately. There is no learning curve to spend here.

## Mobile

390px is the primary layout, not an afterthought. A manager checks pace from a car more often than from a desk. Design at phone width first; desktop is the roomier case.

At 390px: cards single-column, buttons full-width with 44px minimum touch target, metadata stacked, long hashes wrapped, no horizontal overflow.

## Never

No localStorage or sessionStorage outside `src/storage/`. No health scores or gauges. No scroll animation or parallax. No badges, streaks, or celebration states. No literal Athena iconography — no owls, columns, laurel, or wings in the mark.
